# Domain 6. Treatment, Intervention, Prevention and Supervision (15%), KN42-KN52. Pool 28, 7 per test.
CAT = "epppTreat"; SEG = "TRT"
Q = [
("Across many psychotherapy outcome studies, which factor accounts for MORE outcome variance than differences between treatment models?",
 "The therapeutic alliance", ["The therapist's theoretical school", "The number of years in practice", "The session length"],
 "Meta-analyses (Wampold, Norcross) find the alliance reliably predicts outcome, while differences between bona fide treatments are small. Years of experience show only weak links to outcome."),
("In exposure and response prevention for OCD, the response that the client prevents is the:",
 "Compulsive ritual", ["Obsessive thought", "Feeling of anxiety", "Feared situation"],
 "Clients face feared cues (exposure) and then refrain from the rituals that relieve anxiety (response prevention), so anxiety falls without them. Obsessions and anxiety are allowed to occur."),
("A client in the precontemplation stage of change is BEST helped by:",
 "Raising awareness of the problem", ["A detailed relapse plan", "Rewards for staying sober", "Setting a quit date now"],
 "In the transtheoretical model, precontemplators do not yet see a problem, so consciousness raising and feedback fit best. Setting a quit date suits preparation, and relapse plans suit maintenance."),
("The central dialectic that Linehan's dialectical behavior therapy asks therapist and client to balance is between:",
 "Acceptance and change", ["Thoughts and feelings", "Past and present", "Insight and action"],
 "DBT pairs acceptance strategies (validation, mindfulness) with change strategies (behavioral analysis, skills training), holding both at once. It was built for chronically suicidal, self-harming clients and delivered through skills groups, individual therapy and phone coaching."),
("In Bowen's family systems theory, the ability to keep one's own thinking separate from family emotion is:",
 "Differentiation of self", ["Triangulation", "Enmeshment", "Emotional cutoff"],
 "Bowen saw differentiation as the core goal: a well-differentiated person stays connected without being driven by the family's anxiety. Emotional cutoff is managing that anxiety by distancing."),
("A structural family therapist who joins the family and redraws boundaries between parents and children follows:",
 "Minuchin", ["Satir", "Haley", "Whitaker"],
 "Minuchin's structural therapy focuses on hierarchy, subsystems and boundaries, with techniques such as joining and enactments. Haley's strategic therapy relies on directives and paradoxical tasks."),
("In Caplan's model, a consultant who helps a teacher whose own reactions are interfering with work with one student provides:",
 "Consultee-centered case consultation", ["Client-centered case consultation", "Program-centered administrative consultation", "Consultee-centered administrative consultation"],
 "Consultee-centered case consultation targets the consultee's lack of knowledge, skill, confidence or objectivity about a case. Client-centered case consultation focuses on assessing and planning for the client."),
("Holland's theory of career choice predicts job satisfaction from the fit between:",
 "Personality type and work setting", ["Ability and pay level", "Values and job location", "Age and career stage"],
 "Holland's RIASEC model holds that people in work environments matching their personality type are more satisfied and stable. Career stages across the lifespan are Super's focus."),
("Super's life-span theory treats career development as:",
 "Unfolding across life stages", ["A one-time match of traits", "Driven by chance events", "Set by early childhood needs"],
 "Super described stages of growth, exploration, establishment, maintenance and disengagement, with self-concept guiding choices across the life span. Roe linked career choice to early parent-child relations."),
("A psychologist plans video therapy with a client in another state. She should FIRST confirm that:",
 "She may legally practice there", ["The platform has video filters", "The client has a smartphone", "The session can be recorded"],
 "Jurisdiction generally follows the client's location, so the psychologist must be licensed or authorized there, for example under PSYPACT where both states take part. Security and informed consent follow."),
("In secondary prevention, a psychologist's main aim is to:",
 "Catch problems early", ["Stop problems from starting", "Reduce long-term disability", "Change public policy"],
 "Caplan's secondary prevention is early identification and prompt treatment to shorten a disorder's duration. Primary prevention stops new cases, and tertiary prevention limits disability from an established disorder."),
("A psychotherapy for depression that focuses on grief, role disputes, role transitions and social skill deficits is:",
 "Interpersonal psychotherapy", ["Behavioral activation", "Schema therapy", "Acceptance and commitment therapy"],
 "IPT, from Klerman and Weissman, links mood to current relationships through four problem areas. Behavioral activation schedules rewarding activity, and ACT targets psychological flexibility."),
("A first-line psychological treatment for chronic insomnia in adults is:",
 "CBT for insomnia", ["Sleep hygiene advice alone", "Hypnotic medication alone", "Psychodynamic therapy"],
 "CBT-I, combining stimulus control, sleep restriction and cognitive work, is recommended as first-line care and has more durable effects than hypnotics. Sleep hygiene alone is not effective as a stand-alone treatment."),
("Prolonged exposure therapy for PTSD includes imaginal exposure plus:",
 "In vivo exposure", ["Eye movements", "Interoceptive exposure", "Relaxation training"],
 "Foa's prolonged exposure combines repeated imaginal recounting of the trauma memory with graded in vivo exposure to avoided safe situations. Eye movements are part of EMDR."),
("In acceptance and commitment therapy, defusion techniques aim to help clients:",
 "See thoughts as just thoughts", ["Prove their thoughts are false", "Stop all unwanted thinking", "Uncover childhood conflicts"],
 "Defusion changes the client's relationship to thoughts, such as noticing 'I'm having the thought that...', rather than disputing their content as cognitive restructuring does."),
("In Bernard's discrimination model of supervision, the supervisor moves among three roles: teacher, counselor and:",
 "Consultant", ["Evaluator", "Mentor", "Gatekeeper"],
 "Bernard's model has three roles (teacher, counselor, consultant) applied across focus areas such as intervention, conceptualization and personalization. Evaluation runs through all supervision but is not one of the roles."),
("The integrated developmental model of supervision predicts that a beginning trainee will typically show:",
 "High anxiety and dependence", ["Strong autonomy", "Fluctuating motivation", "Integrated self-awareness"],
 "In Stoltenberg's IDM, Level 1 trainees are motivated but anxious and dependent on the supervisor; Level 2 brings fluctuating confidence and autonomy conflicts, and Level 3 more stable independence."),
("A psychologist designs a vaccination campaign based on perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits and barriers. This applies the:",
 "Health belief model", ["Theory of planned behavior", "Transtheoretical model", "Self-regulation model"],
 "The health belief model predicts health action from perceived threat (susceptibility and severity) weighed against perceived benefits and barriers, with cues to action. The theory of planned behavior uses attitudes, norms and perceived control."),
("For blood-injection-injury phobia with fainting, a technique of tensing body muscles to raise blood pressure is:",
 "Applied tension", ["Applied relaxation", "Systematic desensitization", "Paced diaphragmatic breathing"],
 "Ost's applied tension counters the sudden drop in blood pressure that causes fainting in this phobia. Relaxation would lower blood pressure further."),
("Motivational interviewing responds to a client's arguments against change by:",
 "Rolling with resistance", ["Confronting the denial", "Listing the risks again", "Setting firm limits"],
 "MI avoids arguing for change, which tends to strengthen the client's opposing statements; it reflects, reframes and elicits the client's own reasons for change. Direct confrontation raises resistance."),
("Under managed care, a psychologist's treatment plan is MOST often limited by:",
 "Session caps and authorization", ["The client's diagnosis only", "State licensing rules", "The therapist's own model"],
 "Managed care controls costs through utilization review, preauthorization and limits on sessions, which shape treatment length and choice. Diagnosis matters to coverage but is not the main limit on planning."),
("A treatment found effective in a tightly controlled trial is tested under ordinary clinic conditions. This second study examines:",
 "Effectiveness", ["Efficacy", "Feasibility", "Fidelity"],
 "Efficacy is benefit under ideal research conditions; effectiveness is benefit in routine practice with typical clients and therapists. Fidelity is whether a treatment was delivered as designed."),
("In the transtheoretical model, using relaxation in place of a cigarette when stressed is the change process called:",
 "Counterconditioning", ["Stimulus control", "Self-reevaluation", "Dramatic relief"],
 "Counterconditioning substitutes a healthier behavior for the problem behavior. Stimulus control removes cues for the habit, and self-reevaluation and dramatic relief are experiential processes used earlier in change."),
("Which of Yalom's therapeutic factors involves members reliving and revising early family patterns in the group?",
 "Corrective recapitulation", ["Universality", "Imparting information", "Instillation of hope"],
 "Yalom lists the corrective recapitulation of the primary family group among his therapeutic factors: members re-experience and revise family patterns. Universality is learning that others share one's problems."),
("A school district teaches social-emotional skills to all students, whether or not they show problems. This is a:",
 "Universal intervention", ["Selective intervention", "Indicated intervention", "Tertiary intervention"],
 "The Institute of Medicine framework classes prevention as universal (everyone), selective (higher-risk groups) and indicated (people with early signs). Teaching every student is universal."),
("Psychoeducation for families of people with schizophrenia is used MAINLY to reduce:",
 "Relapse and rehospitalization", ["The need for any medication", "Positive symptoms on its own", "The person's IQ decline"],
 "Family psychoeducation teaches about the illness, communication and problem solving, and lowers relapse and hospitalization when added to medication. It does not replace antipsychotic treatment."),
("A psychologist adapts an evidence-based treatment for a cultural group while keeping its core components. This approach is called:",
 "Cultural adaptation", ["Treatment drift", "Eclecticism", "Program termination"],
 "Cultural adaptation modifies language, content and delivery to fit a group's values while preserving the active ingredients. Treatment drift is unplanned departure from a protocol."),
("Rogers' term for the therapist's genuineness, with inner experience matching outward expression, is:",
 "Congruence", ["Accurate empathy", "Positive regard", "Self-actualization"],
 "Congruence is one of Rogers' core conditions, alongside unconditional positive regard and accurate empathy. Self-actualization is the growth tendency the conditions release."),
]
